evidence.text:
- the Trade Marks Act 1995 (January 1996, the cost of a trade mark up 36.59%): rushes of -0.304 (Australians) and -0.202 (overseas applicants), 2.778% of placebos each: falls before a rise, the Act's pause (B37), not a price;
- overseas applicants' standard patents before the 15.31% cut of January 1998: a rush of 0.147 (2.778%), filing brought forward before a cut, against a price; their step after it (0.096, 5.556%) is at the edge;
- the Designs Act 2003 (June 2004, a design's filing fee up 122.2%): overseas applicants' designs stepped up 0.334 (none of 72), against a price (the Act, B37); their rush (0.253, 12.5%) is within the ordinary;
- Australians' designs after the 15.38% rise of July 1988: a step of -0.136 (2.778% of 36 placebos), the only one in a price's direction.
